WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.230
You're glued to your TV,

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.972
watching your favorite show,

00:00:03.972 --> 00:00:05.688
and there's a heart-pounding moment

00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:08.940
where a beloved character needs CPR.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.430
Oh, man!

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:11.823
What's gonna happen?

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.165
Oh, they're fine?

00:00:14.165 --> 00:00:17.691
That's because it's TV, and not real life.

00:00:17.691 --> 00:00:20.952
In reality, CPR is a way to keep a victim

00:00:20.952 --> 00:00:25.581
of cardiac arrest alive before
additional treatment arrives.

00:00:25.581 --> 00:00:28.210
However, survival rates are much lower

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.650
than what's shown on TV.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.198
For instance, one 2018 paper included

00:00:33.198 --> 00:00:38.198
18,000 hospitalized cardiac
arrest cases who received CPR.

00:00:38.691 --> 00:00:42.821
Overall, only 28.5% of the adults survived

00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:45.350
to eventually leave the hospital.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.220
Now let's compare that
to TV medical dramas.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.952
A team of researchers sat down and watched

00:00:49.952 --> 00:00:53.466
91 episodes of Grey's Anatomy and House.

00:00:53.466 --> 00:00:56.900
They discovered that about 70% of people

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.580
who received CPR survived on the TV shows.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.330
That's over double the survival rate

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.541
compared to actual reality.

00:01:05.541 --> 00:01:08.790
And of course, Hollywood is
allowed to bend the truth

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.592
for the sake of suspense,
but in this case,

00:01:11.592 --> 00:01:14.371
this misinformation can be misleading.

00:01:14.371 --> 00:01:18.472
Many doctors actually opt
out of CPR for themselves.

00:01:18.472 --> 00:01:22.038
There are even cases of
doctors tattooing their chests

00:01:22.038 --> 00:01:25.551
to make sure that they don't receive CPR.

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:28.250
It may sound paranoid, but doctors know

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.120
even if you beat the odds and survive,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.341
your quality of life
may never be the same.

00:01:33.341 --> 00:01:36.700
That's because of how CPR actually works.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.503
CPR stands for
cardiopulmonary resuscitation.

00:01:40.503 --> 00:01:44.328
When the heart stops, breathing
air into the person's lungs

00:01:44.328 --> 00:01:46.552
and pressing down on their chest

00:01:46.552 --> 00:01:49.360
keeps oxygen flowing to the brain,

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.831
and it may restart their heart.

00:01:51.831 --> 00:01:54.764
But what they usually
don't show on TV dramas

00:01:54.764 --> 00:01:58.219
is that CPR can fracture your breastbone,

00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:01.680
crack ribs, and bruise your lungs.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.691
These types of injuries are common

00:02:03.691 --> 00:02:06.061
because your heart is inside your ribcage

00:02:06.061 --> 00:02:08.510
and surrounded by other organs.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.152
So in order to make an impact on it,

00:02:10.152 --> 00:02:13.731
you have to compress the chest
by about five centimeters,

00:02:13.731 --> 00:02:16.608
and of course, our ribs aren't designed

00:02:16.608 --> 00:02:19.380
to withstand that kind of force.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.662
And even worse, brain cells start dying

00:02:22.662 --> 00:02:25.489
six minutes after the heart stops.

00:02:25.489 --> 00:02:28.171
So people who don't receive CPR right away

00:02:28.171 --> 00:02:31.091
are at risk of permanent brain damage.

00:02:31.091 --> 00:02:34.776
For example, one study in
China found that about a third

00:02:34.776 --> 00:02:38.520
of survivors had some
level of brain damage.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.820
Now, just because TV shows
get most of this wrong

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.231
isn't the problem, it's
the fact that we believe

00:02:44.231 --> 00:02:47.432
a lot more of what we
see on TV than we should.

00:02:47.432 --> 00:02:52.010
When 269 people were asked
where they got information

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:57.010
about CPR, almost half said
they used TV shows as a source.

00:02:58.021 --> 00:03:01.952
One survey found that nearly
a quarter of older people

00:03:01.952 --> 00:03:06.460
believed that 90% of CPR cases survived,

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.461
which may lead them to
opt for CPR themselves,

00:03:10.461 --> 00:03:14.171
and that's really problematic
for people over 65

00:03:14.171 --> 00:03:18.400
who have a lower survival
rate than younger patients.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.511
So when it comes to medical advice,

00:03:20.511 --> 00:03:23.913
and I can't believe I have to say this,

00:03:23.913 --> 00:03:27.360
don't depend on the doctors on TV.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.203
Ask a real doctor.

